fact WorkItem(id: "WI-884", description: some(value: "BACK THE SIBLING PRIMITIVE SORTS' DECLARED-BUT-DEAD OPERATIONS — WI-881's sibling audit, MEASURED by driving each one from an operation body on a program that LOADS CLEAN. EIGHT operations across two sorts die `OperationBodyMissing`, in exactly the shape WI-881 found on `Float`: declared on the sort, exempted from the load-time backing check because the carrier is a host `Implementation.target` (`check_provider_operations` skips it wholesale — WI-880), and backed by nothing. THE LIST. `anthill.prelude.Int64`: `minValue()`, `maxValue()` — both unbacked, and `int64.anthill`'s own `constraint in_bounds: gte(?n, minValue), lte(?n, maxValue)` is stated OVER THEM (that constraint is separately inert and WI-882 deletes it, so the two questions are independent). `anthill.prelude.String`: `isEmpty`, `contains`, `indexOf`, `replace`, `trim`, `split` — six unbacked out of the sort's fourteen non-ordering operations; `concat`/`length`/`startsWith`/`endsWith`/`substring`/`toUpper`/`toLower`/`repeat` are registered and `padRight` has a real body. CLEAN: `anthill.prelude.BigInt` — every declared operation runs (`compare`/`gt`/`gte`/`lt`/`lte`/`max`/`min` via WI-876's `operation_map`, `to_bigint`/`to_int`/`to_float` as builtins). `Bool` was not in WI-881's audit scope and should be checked here too. THE WORK IS THE SAME MECHANICAL SHAPE WI-881 USED — add the host function to `host_fn_by_key`'s closed registry and name it in the carrier's `operation_map` — but each one carries a SEMANTICS DECISION that `Float`'s did not, so it is not a copy-paste: `indexOf` returns `-1` when absent (BYTE offset or CHAR offset? the declaration says `Int64` and nothing more, and Rust's `str::find` gives BYTES while a user reading `substring(s, start, end)` will assume the same unit as `length` — so `length`'s existing unit has to be measured first and the three made to agree); `split` must BUILD a `List[T = String]` (a host function returning a constructed list value, which none of the current `host_fn_by_key` entries do — `bigint_to_int` builds an `Option` and is the pattern to follow); `trim` must say WHICH whitespace (Rust's `str::trim` is Unicode `White_Space`, not ASCII). TWO OF THE EIGHT MAY NOT NEED HOST CODE AT ALL, and WI-881 settled the same question for `Float.recip`/`tau`: `string.anthill` already carries `rule isEmpty(?s) <=> eq(length(?s), 0)` untagged, and that IS the definition — `[simp]` makes it run with no host function (a `[simp]` equation is executable by INLINING; an untagged one is inert, see kernel-language.md 'Equational rules'). `contains` has only the partial law `contains(?s, \"\") <=> true`, which is not a definition, so it needs the host. Settle each of the six `String` laws the way WI-881 settled `Float`'s four, rather than blanket-mapping. `Int64.minValue`/`maxValue` are unambiguous (`i64::MIN`/`i64::MAX`) and are the two to do first. ACCEPTANCE: every operation `int64.anthill`, `string.anthill` and `bool.anthill` declare either runs or is deleted from the sort; a test drives each newly-backed operation; `indexOf`/`substring`/`length` agree on their index unit and the spec says which; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. REFERENCE: WI-881 (the same defect on `Float`, and the per-law settlement method), WI-876 (the `operation_map` channel), WI-880 (the host-carrier exemption that hides all of these at load)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-30T23:02:23Z"))
